# Anonymoux Livecast

A production dashboard for Decart's full Lucy model family, built on the
official `@decartai/sdk`. Three studios, one console:

- **Realtime** â€” live webcam transformation over WebRTC (edit, virtual
  try-on, restyle)
- **Video Studio** â€” async video jobs via the Queue API (edit, try-on,
  restyle, image-to-video, text-to-video)
- **Image Studio** â€” synchronous image jobs via the Process API (edit,
  text-to-image)

## Models wired in

| Studio | Model ID | Decart model | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Realtime | `realtime.lucy-2.5` | `lucy-2.5` | General live edit, prompt + optional reference image |
| Realtime | `realtime.lucy-vton-3` | `lucy-vton-3` | Live garment swap |
| Realtime | `realtime.lucy-restyle-2` | `lucy-restyle-2` | Live whole-scene restyle |
| Video Studio | `video.lucy-2.1` | `lucy-2.1` | Async video edit |
| Video Studio | `video.lucy-vton-3` | `lucy-vton-3` | Async video try-on (+ reference garment) |
| Video Studio | `video.lucy-restyle-2` | `lucy-restyle-2` | Async video restyle |
| Video Studio | `video.lucy-pro-i2v` | `lucy-pro-i2v` | Image â†’ video |
| Video Studio | `video.lucy-pro-t2v` | `lucy-pro-t2v` | Text â†’ video |
| Image Studio | `image.lucy-image-2` | `lucy-image-2` | Image edit (+ optional reference) |
| Image Studio | `image.lucy-pro-t2i` | `lucy-pro-t2i` | Text â†’ image |

The full mapping lives in one place â€” `MODEL_REGISTRY` in `server.js` â€” so
adding, removing, or renaming a model is a single-file change. The frontend
never hardcodes a Decart model string; it only knows the UI-facing ids
above, which `GET /health` also echoes back for reference.

## How it works

### Realtime (WebRTC, browser â†” Decart directly)

`server.js` mints a short-lived (5 min) client token via the SDK's
documented `client.tokens.create()`, scoped to the selected realtime model.
The browser uses that token to open its own `client.realtime.connect()`
session straight to Decart's edge network â€” video never touches this
server for realtime sessions, only the token-mint round trip does.

### Video Studio (async Queue API â€” needs the permanent key)

Queue jobs (video edit/restyle/try-on/i2v/t2v) require the permanent
`DECART_API_KEY`, so they can't run client-side without exposing it. The
browser instead uploads to this server as `multipart/form-data`, which:

1. `POST /api/video/submit` â€” forwards the file(s) + prompt to
   `client.queue.submit()`, returns `{ jobId }`
2. `GET /api/video/status/:jobId` â€” the browser polls this every 3s via
   `client.queue.status()`
3. `GET /api/video/result/:jobId` â€” once `status === "completed"`, the
   browser fetches this to stream the finished video back, via
   `client.queue.result()`

Uploads are held in memory only (`multer.memoryStorage()`) and forwarded
straight through â€” nothing is written to disk on this server.

### Image Studio (sync Process API â€” needs the permanent key)

Same reasoning as Queue: `POST /api/image/process` forwards the upload (or,
for text-to-image, just the prompt) to `client.process()` and streams the
resulting image straight back in one response â€” no polling needed.

### Honest telemetry (Realtime tab)

- **Status** â€” from the SDK's documented `connectionChange` event.
- **Frames Processed** â€” counted client-side from the output `<video>`
  element using the browser's real `requestVideoFrameCallback` API.
- **Generation Time** â€” from the SDK's documented `generationTick` event.
- **Latency** â€” the Decart SDK does not expose a per-frame latency metric,
  so this is shown as `â€”` rather than a fabricated number.

## Local development

```bash
npm install
cp .env.example .env
# edit .env and set DECART_API_KEY
npm run dev
```

Open `http://localhost:10000`.

## Deploying to Render

1. Push this repo to GitHub.
2. In Render, click **New â†’ Blueprint** and point it at the repo â€” it will
   pick up `render.yaml` automatically. Alternatively, create a **New â†’ Web
   Service** manually with:
   - Build command: `npm install`
   - Start command: `npm start`
   - Health check path: `/health`
3. Set the `DECART_API_KEY` environment variable in the Render dashboard
   (marked `sync: false` in `render.yaml`, so Render prompts for it rather
   than committing it to the repo).
4. Deploy. Your app will be live at `https://<service-name>.onrender.com`.

Render's free tier has a request body/timeout ceiling that's fine for
image uploads but worth checking against your expected video upload sizes
if you plan to accept large source clips (Decart's own cap is 300MB for
video edit/restyle inputs).

## Security notes

- `DECART_API_KEY` is read only from `process.env` server-side and is
  never sent to the browser â€” only short-lived realtime tokens are, and
  only for the Realtime tab.
- Video and image uploads for Video Studio / Image Studio pass through
  this server (in memory, never persisted to disk) because the Queue and
  Process APIs require the permanent key. This is unavoidable without
  giving every visitor your permanent key â€” treat `/api/video/submit` and
  `/api/image/process` as billable endpoints and consider adding your own
  auth/rate-limiting in front of them before a public launch, since as
  shipped they're open to any caller who can reach the server.
- Client-side validation caps uploads by type and size per model (8MB
  images, 300MB video). Decart also enforces its own limits server-side.
- Set `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` in production to restrict which origins can call
  `POST /token`. Consider a matching allowlist / auth layer for the
  `/api/*` routes if you deploy this publicly, since those aren't scoped
  by `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` (that only governs CORS on cross-origin requests,
  not who can call the server directly).

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `index.html` | Frontend console â€” Realtime / Video Studio / Image Studio tabs, all Decart SDK + fetch integration |
| `server.js` | Express backend â€” realtime token minting, Queue proxy, Process proxy, model registry |
| `package.json` | Dependencies and start script |
| `.env.example` | Environment variable template |
| `render.yaml` | Render deployment blueprint |
